<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Dürkheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Dürkheim is a spa town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haardt">Haardt</a> hills on the German Wine Route. In the 18th century it became the residence of the counts, later princes, of Leiningen-Hardenburg, whose palace was destroyed by French revolutionary troops in 1794. It is known for its wine, its salt springs and the Wurstmarkt, one of the largest wine festivals in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-durkheim.jpg" alt="Bad Dürkheim" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: --Immanuel Giel 09:37, 21 May 2007 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gasse_Bad_Duerkheim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
